From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: TheDBP <TheDBP@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 14:06:05 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] bpf: do not abort when a pinned map cannot be opened

This is what has been killing system_server on ether 20.0. The tombstone:

  pid: 7388, tid: 7541, name: WifiHandlerThre  >>> system_server <<<
  signal 6 (SIGABRT)
  #00 abort+164                                              libc.so
  #01 android::bpf::BpfMap<unsigned int, StatsValue>::BpfMap(char const*, unsigned int)+436
  #02 android::bpf::bpfGetIfaceStats(char const*, Stats*)    libnetworkstats.so
  #03 android::getIfaceStat(_JNIEnv*, _jclass*, _jstring*, int)

msm8992 runs a 3.10 kernel with no BPF syscall at all, so mapRetrieve() can
never succeed. The first time anything asks for per-interface network stats --
in practice the WiFi handler thread, early in boot -- the constructor aborts
and takes system_server with it. Fifteen restarts per boot, then RescueParty.

The callers are already written to cope. bpfGetIfaceStats() does:

    BpfMapRO<uint32_t, StatsValue> ifaceStatsMap(IFACE_STATS_MAP_PATH);
    if (!ifaceStatsMap.isValid()) { ... return ret; }

It simply never gets to run that check, because the constructor aborts first.
So this leaves the fd invalid and lets the existing error handling do its job.

Deliberately narrow: only the pinned-path constructor changes. The
createMap() constructor still aborts, because a process that sets out to
create a map (bpfloader) genuinely cannot continue without one, and that is
not a path ether reaches. The key/value size checks are untouched -- they are
already gated behind isAtLeastKernelVersion(4, 14, 0).

Complements the existing Connectivity patch, which fixed the socket-tagging
path (BpfHandler) but not the stats path. Same cost as documented there: no
per-UID accounting, no BPF firewall. The real fix is still a kernel with eBPF.
---
 common/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfMap.h | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/common/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfMap.h b/common/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfMap.h
index 2bee2ee4..1f707fe6 100644
--- a/common/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfMap.h
+++ b/common/native/bpf_headers/include/bpf/BpfMap.h
@@ -50,7 +50,14 @@ class BpfMap {
     // flag must be within BPF_OBJ_FLAG_MASK, ie. 0, BPF_F_RDONLY, BPF_F_WRONLY
     BpfMap<Key, Value>(const char* pathname, uint32_t flags) {
         mMapFd.reset(mapRetrieve(pathname, flags));
-        if (mMapFd < 0) abort();
+        if (mMapFd < 0) {
+            // A kernel with no BPF syscall can never open a pinned map, so this is
+            // an expected outcome on such devices rather than a programming error.
+            // Every caller of this constructor already tests isValid() and degrades
+            // gracefully; aborting takes the whole process down before they can.
+            ALOGW("BpfMap: pinned map %s unavailable; leaving map invalid", pathname);
+            return;
+        }
         if (isAtLeastKernelVersion(4, 14, 0)) {
             if (bpfGetFdKeySize(mMapFd) != sizeof(Key)) abort();
             if (bpfGetFdValueSize(mMapFd) != sizeof(Value)) abort();
